Armed forces spend three days working together to break up murder case in Kandal Stung district, Kandal province
(Kandal): After finding the bodies of the victims in Kandal Steung District, Kandal Province, the Kandal Provincial Gendarmerie, the National Gendarmerie Justice Commission, the Prey Veng Provincial Gendarmerie and Phnom Penh Capital City have cooperated to split the murder case successfully after Investigate for 3 days.
The murder happened at 12 midnight on January 12, 2019 at a rice field in Trapeang Bakou village, Trapeang Veng commune, Kandal Steung district, Kandal province. The victim, Khon Savath, 19, lived in a rented room with his mother, Nuon Nak, 46, in Kraing Trea village, Preah Nipean commune, Kong Pisey district, Kampong Speu province.
It should be noted that after receiving information from the people found the body at the above point on January 30, 2019, the police force of Kandal Stung district and the provincial expertise led by Brigadier General Sam Tola, Commander of the Kandal Provincial Gendarmerie. Arrive at the scene immediately, working diligently on their skills.
In this case, Brigadier General Sam Tola ordered a specialized force to investigate, including Colonel Touch Inan, Deputy Commander of the Justice and Special Forces for 3 days to identify the suspects and seek arrest. The suspects arrived the next day.
The achievement of the above good results is due to the coordination of the procedure from the representatives of the Prosecutor's Office of Kandal, Prey Veng and Phnom Penh in the expansion of territorial jurisdiction and with good cooperation between all military police. Class order.
Currently, the four suspects have been charged by the specialized department and sent to the Kandal Provincial Court for legal action. Israeli man arrested in Svay Rieng for drug offenses
(Svay Rieng): An Israeli man was detained by a joint police force in a guesthouse in Mefleung village, Svay Rieng commune, Svay Rieng city, along with some drugs and exhibits, and sent for questioning at the Svay Rieng Provincial Police to prepare a case. The matter is sent to court.
According to Lt. Col. Keo Sotha, Deputy Commissioner for Anti-Drug Planning, the operation to crack down on drug offenses was conducted on February 3, 2019 at the Jinglong Guesthouse after the competent authorities received information from the innkeeper. That there is suspicion of foreigners staying in rented rooms.
Lt. Col. Keo Sotha said that with the coordination of Mr. Kong Chan Khemrith, Prosecutor of the Provincial Court, the Anti-Drug Bureau, in cooperation with the Immigration Office, Provincial Health Department, Svay Rieng City Police Inspectorate and relevant local authorities cracked down on suspects. One person was drugged and used in drugs and sent for questioning and urine test for marijuana.
Currently, the case is being investigated by the competent authorities and the case is being processed. For the above plants and chemicals, the authorities have prepared to send a part to the National Authority for forensic examination.
International Analysis: The Dangers of Trusting US Powers, Reminding US Allies in Asia
(World): Before the US betrayed the Kurds in Syria, the United States also abandoned the Cambodian and Vietnamese people in the 1970s, letting go of fate.
US President Donald Trump's decision to withdraw all troops from Syria serves as an important new reminder to those who believe in US assurances and promises that they are in danger.
With the United States behind the funding, training and arming, the Kurds would have to pay back the United States with blood. True, Trump fulfilled his campaign promise by withdrawing US troops from Syria, but to the Kurds, it was as if Trump was stabbing them in the back. The Kurds have spoken openly many times in the world media.
This is a lesson for South Korea, Japan and Taiwan, which are close allies of this big brother, who need to watch this development closely because it is the most alarming breach of trust. So the other economies in the Asia-Pacific region that the United States is comforting by fraud are likely to be US tools just to curb China's growing influence.
One thing that the governments of Seoul and Tokyo should not forget is that immediately after President Trump met with North Korean leader Kim Jong Un in Singapore in June, he canceled a previous military exercise. Between South Korean and US forces a long time ago. This is an example of what the United States can do immediately that the Seoul government could not have noticed.
Now the question is, is the United States a reliable ally? It is becoming an important question and it is not clear what the governments of Seoul and Tokyo understand. Trust it or not, we can observe the recent actions of both countries. In fact, South Korea seems to be softening its stance toward North Korea, while Tokyo has also signaled its intention to improve good relations with Beijing.
In this regard, Taiwan should also note the danger that awaits it if Taiwan continues to allow the United States to use itself as a pawn to control its strategy on China. The Trump administration's abandonment of its allies in Syria is a worrying reminder for other US allies.
The facts are indisputable when it comes to promises and assurances that the United States has had the most embarrassing history of failing to retake the archipelago in the South China Sea, which Japan recaptured from China. The Cairo Conference Agreement of November 1943, the abandonment of Hungary, when Soviet troops entered to crush the insurgency in 1956.
Vietnam, once dragged by Washington to oppose Beijing's ambitions in the South China Sea, should also be remembered after the United States abandoned its ally until the end of the Indochina War in 1975.
It is clear that many people who went through those events will never forget the image of a US Marine helicopter evacuating its citizens from the roof of the US Embassy in Saigon on April 29, 1975, while Vietnam was Fall into the flames of war. But something even more horrific and embarrassing was happening in Phnom Penh three weeks before the Vietnam War.
Recall that in April 1975, the United States instigated General Lon Nol to overthrow Prince Norodom Sihanouk in a coup, but five years later the United States abandoned Cambodia, allowing it to fall into the era of Democratic Kampuchea. .
The shameful story of US action was leaked by Prince Sirik Matak, then Cambodian Deputy Prime Minister. In fact, Ambassador Dean ordered him in a convoy to evacuate and grant him asylum, while Prime Minister Lon Nol had already fled to Hawaii.
Prince Sirik Matak responded to the actions of the United States by writing, "Your Excellency and dear friends, I would like to thank you very much for your letter and your passport to give me the freedom to flee." "But I really can not leave the country with such cowardice."
But remember, if I die here in the country I love, it would be a terrible thing, because we are all born to die one day, but I admit one mistake I made was to believe in Mr., American.
Please be informed that this reply message was written, distributed and read with ridicule in the territory of Washington State Government. The author of this letter was later captured by Khmer Rouge soldiers and killed. But reports say he was shot in the abdomen and left for dead after suffering three days of injuries, while other sources say he was beheaded.
Want to know if the current US President Donald Trump read the letter before ordering the withdrawal of troops from Syria. If you have read this, you may want to consider carefully before making such a decision.
The signing ceremony of the Memorandum of Understanding on Cooperation between the Royal University of Phnom Penh and the National Institute of Diplomacy and International Relations on February 1, 2019 at the Institute of Foreign Languages at the Royal University of Phnom Penh.
At the same time, Mr. Chet Chealy, Rector of the Royal University of Phnom Penh (RUPP) said that the benefit for students is that students can have an internship on the actual study that has a high capacity in addition to the internship. I gained good experience and knowledge from speakers from the National Institute of Diplomacy and International Relations.
Mr. Chet Chealy added that another benefit is for the Royal University of Phnom Penh, especially for the field of international relations, the university is more confident in training students after a discussion on student capacity building with the Institute. National Diplomacy and International Relations. In addition, there is also a discussion on exchanging speakers with each other to provide new knowledge to students.
Mr. Chhiv Yi Seang, Undersecretary of State and President of the National Institute of Diplomacy and International Relations, said that although the National Institute of Diplomacy and International Relations is newly established, the Institute also has relations and partnerships with many national and international universities. In order to strengthen the capacity of students.
Mr. Chhiv Yi Seang further stated that the National Institute of Diplomacy and International Relations has decided to sign a Memorandum of Understanding on Cooperation with the Royal University of Phnom Penh at this time because of the view that RUPP is a university. The oldest in Cambodia and has a lot of experience and human resources. In particular, there is a teaching department similar to the National Institute of Diplomacy and International Relations.
The President of the National Institute of Diplomacy and International Relations said that for university graduates who wish to work for the Cambodian Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, it is easy for them to become diplomats in the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.
Mr. Chhiv Yi Seang said that the current cooperation is to help university students gain diplomatic access and exchange experiences and internships at the National Institute of Diplomacy and International Relations. But for students in other fields, there are the same opportunities as students studying foreign languages, but there are only some conditions that are in the signing of the Memorandum of Understanding now.
It should be noted that, in general, the Institute of Foreign Languages at the Royal University of Phnom Penh is taught in many languages, including English, French, Chinese, Thai, Korean and Japanese, and receives thousands of students each year. Of the thousands of students, more than 100 were selected by the Cambodian Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation for further study.
