The South American country has been in decline in recent years as the political crisis worsens, fueled by rising inflation, power outages and food and medicine shortages. More than three million Venezuelans have left the country in recent years. What is behind the Venezuelan earthquake?
* Who is the President? This is a difficult question to answer in most countries, but in Venezuela many people want to know exactly what will happen after the dramatic event on the 23rd. January 2019.
Maduro was re-elected for a second term in the most controversial election in May 2018 as the opposition boycotted the election results.
Many opposition candidates have been barred from running in the election, while others have been jailed or forced to flee the country for fear of imprisonment. Opposition parties have stated they will not run in the by-elections. The election results were not recognized by the National Assembly, which is controlled by the Venezuelan opposition.
* Why was the crisis so hot? After being re-elected in early May 2018, Maduro announced that he would stay in power for a while. Remaining in the first mandate, then sworn in in the second mandate on January 10, 2019.
According to Articles 233 and 333 of the Constitution of Venezuela, the legislature states that in the event of such a situation, the President of the National Assembly assumes the position of Acting President. That is why Guaido declared himself acting president on January 23, 2019.
In Venezuela, as anti-government protesters hailed Guaidó's movement, government officials said they would protect the president from "imperialist threats".
* Who can break the deadlock? Security forces are seen as key players in resolving the crisis. To this day, they remain loyal to Maduro, who has encouraged them by raising their salaries frequently, as well as giving key positions and industrial plants to senior military officials.
* What is the biggest challenge? The biggest challenge that Venezuela is currently facing is inflation. According to a study by the opposition-controlled National Assembly, the annual inflation rate reached 13,000,000% in 12 months. At the end of 2018, commodity prices doubled on average every 19 days. This has made it difficult for many Venezuelans to buy goods such as food and other products.
