BEIJING (Reuters) - At the Paiyun-Guangzhou International Airport in southern China's Guangdong Province, a complete human body scanner was recently launched to enhance security. This is the first airport in China to use this type of body scanning technology.
"It only takes four to five seconds for passengers to get through this scanner," said Xu Yu, an official at the Paiyun International Airport Security Control Unit. In the past, we had to have security guards on hand to scan by hand and take about ten seconds more. ”
In case the scanner finds suspicious objects, the security staff will check another floor.
"Passengers do not have to worry about the scanner's radio waves affecting their health, as they emit weaker radio waves than mobile phones," said Xu Yuyu, an official at the Paiyun International Airport Security Control Unit. In addition, this machine also saves passengers time and energy, and also allows them to travel more easily if we compare with the previous control.
"This new control method is much easier to use and faster than the old method," said Long, a flight attendant. (China CCTV) Preah Vihear Provincial Road Safety Commission Disseminates Road Traffic Law "Today, Tomorrow, No Traffic Accidents"
