Japan will help establish a Cambodian cultural village in its country and will further strengthen the capacity of Cambodian workers in the construction sector
(Phnom Penh): The Cambodia-Japan Cooperation Association has informed Mr. Ith Sam Heng, Minister of Labor and Vocational Training, that he will help establish a Cambodian cultural village in his country, but must first obtain the consent of the Cambodian side. It will also help strengthen the capacity of Cambodian construction workers.
This confirmation was made during a working meeting between Minister Ith Sam Heng and Mr. Fumio Yamada, President of the Cambodia-Japan Cooperation Association on the morning of February 1, 2019 at the Ministry of Labor.
In response to this request, Minister Ith Sam Heng agreed to establish this cultural village to facilitate the gathering of relatives of workers and the general Cambodian population there.
In response, Minister Ith Sam Heng expressed his satisfaction with the remarks made by the President of the Cambodia-Japan Cooperation Association, who introduced construction investors to Cambodia.
The Minister added that in March 2019, Cambodia will sign with the Japanese side in Japan on a cooperation agreement on skilled workers. He said that more than 9,000 workers in Japan hope to double the number next year, so workers also need time to train their skills to do their jobs better. Even better.
It should be noted that the Ministry of Labor is currently cooperating with professional officials of Japan to review and prepare cooperation agreements with each other in order to increase the labor market and job opportunities for the people of Cambodia, Japan has begun to develop a plan. New to increase the flow of labor from other countries and to be implemented during April 2019 to solve the shortage of skilled labor in Japan.
