US spies report from Iran, but contrary to Trump's wishes, the president was expelled to study
WASHINGTON (Reuters) - US President Donald Trump has slammed his intelligence agency for using de-escalation to force all US spies back to school after releasing a report contradicting his claims. . This is according to the release by CNN on the evening of Thursday, January 31, 2019.
Speaking on Twitter this Wednesday, Donald Trump said: "When I took office as President of the United States, Iran caused unrest throughout the Middle East ... but after the disbandment of the icy Iran nuclear deal. Things are getting better. "So be careful of Iran and all intelligence chiefs should go back to school."
Donald Trump's reaction came after US intelligence officials on Tuesday told the Senate that they did not believe Iran was currently developing nuclear weapons, in stark contrast to Trump's claims. He said Iran was a nuclear threat. The General Commission of the National Police calls on enterprises and Borey owners who have not yet registered to use private security agencies to hurry up and register.
(Phnom Penh): The General Commission of the National Police, through the Department of Private Security Management, on the evening of January 31, 2019, called on business owners and Borey owners who have not yet registered to use private security agencies to hurry to register. Legal of the Ministry of Interior and the General Commission of the National Police and related units.
Lt. Gen. Lim Sokharasmey, Director of the Private Security Management Department, at the Closing Ceremony of the 11th Private Security Agency Training Course, said, The law allows from the Capital-Provincial Hall as soon as possible.
The director of this department stressed that if all the enterprises and establishments continue to be strong in the beginning of 2019, the working group of the General Commission of the National Police will investigate and apply discipline to any enterprises and establishments that have not applied for a permit and continue to act illegally. Determined by a fine of 10 million riels and build a case to be sent to court to be punished according to law.
Lt. Gen. Lim Sokharaksmey encourages all private security agencies to report to the local authorities any crimes that occur at the target they are guarding, and all information will be kept confidential and provided. Incentives are also encouraged because private security agencies are a force to be reckoned with.
However, General Lim Sokharaksmey also explained that the word "auxiliary force" is to remember that it is not the authority, so do not go beyond its role and to facilitate cooperation with the authorities at all times, he asserted. That any obstruction or performance of duties beyond its duties may lead to another offense.
General Chhum Socheat, a spokesman for the Ministry of National Defense, confirmed to Fresh News that the report was completely contrary to the position of the Royal Government of Cambodia. He claimed that Cambodia loves peace and will not take any action that affects its independence.
"The Cambodian people love peace, which has been hard to find and through decades of war, we do not take any action that is unconstitutional, leading to another country in chaos," he said. "Cambodia does not need to have foreign military bases on its territory, and Cambodia does not need to amend to allow foreign troops to base as alleged."
Separately, Sok Isan, a spokesman for the ruling party, said that Cambodia firmly adheres to the content of the Cambodian Constitution, which is neutral and does not allow foreign military bases on the territory. He added that in the past, Cambodia has already stated its position that the United States has the right to speak and Cambodia also has the right to confirm the truth, which is our clear position.
The new Article 53 of the Constitution states that Cambodia does not allow foreign military bases on its territory and also does not allow its military bases abroad, except within the framework of the United Nations request. Cambodia reserves the right to accept foreign aid in the form of military equipment, weapons, ammunition, armed forces training and other assistance to defend itself and ensure public order and security within the country.
At the official opening ceremony of the 7th Sea Festival in Koh Kong province on December 15, 2018, Samdech Techo Hun Sen announced that Cambodia has no foreign military bases and that those who are in doubt can actually see it via satellite. He also stated that although he was able to amend the constitution, he would not change the constitution to allow foreign military bases or to send Cambodian troops abroad other than missions with the United Nations.
Earlier, US Vice President Mike Pence, along with some foreign media, expressed suspicion that there were foreign military bases in Cambodia after seeing more Chinese tourists and Chinese investors come to Cambodia. However, this information has already been confirmed and clarified by the head of the Royal Government and the relevant ministries.
The spokesman emphasized that "this liberation is the highest sacrifice of the Cambodian people under the effective leadership and national pride of Samdech Akka Moha Thamma Pothisal Chea Sim, Samdech Akka Moha Ponhea Chakrei Heng Samrin and Samdech Akka Moha Major General Techo Hun Sen, with the voluntary participation of friendly troops from the Socialist Republic of Vietnam. "This is indisputable true history."
The Office of the Spokesman of the Royal Government would like to solemnly declare the protection of the independence and sovereignty of Cambodia, including the care not to let a nation beloved more than its life fall into the colony of any nation or serve the political geography of the interests of any great power. Not one.
It should be noted that the statement of the Royal Government spokesman above after the US Embassy in Cambodia yesterday posted the content on its official Facebook page, referring to the Victory Day on January 7.
The US Embassy recalled that on January 7, 1979, Phnom Penh was liberated from the Khmer Rouge genocidal regime and Cambodia began its journey to a bright and hopeful future.
On this historic 40th anniversary of January, the US Embassy is dedicated to the lives of more than 2 million Cambodians who died during the Khmer Rouge regime and the approximately 5 million survivors of this regime. Suffering both physically and mentally. This is further confirmed by the US Embassy.
