(Phnom Penh): The director of the Phnom Penh Municipal Health Department, Dr. Ngy Mean Heng, stated that drinking sweet drinks can cause four major health risks: obesity, tooth decay, diabetes and heart disease.
This statement was made by the doctor on the occasion of the integration of knowledge on reducing the consumption of sweet drinks through the practice of well-being held at the Tonle Bassac 2 restaurant on January 31, 2019.
The director of the Phnom Penh Municipal Health Department said, "The aim is to make people aware of the challenges of drinking sugary drinks that cause us to get sick: coronary heart disease, which can lead to heart attack and stroke." Diabetes, cancer, cancer, tooth decay, obesity. 90% of the people in Phnom Penh drink this sweet drink a lot.
In addition, the doctor continued, "For the well-being of the people, we have developed a policy to reduce the consumption of sweet drinks, continue to monitor the consumption of sweet drinks, especially among children to promote the health effects caused by drinking sweet drinks. "
At the same time, the participant of this workshop, Mr. Taing Theng, said, "This workshop is very important and I will share the effects of drinking this sweet drink, and I used to drink a lot every day. "But I will cut down on it even more."
(Phnom Penh): The United States Government has provided more than US $ 17 million in assistance to increase and develop freshwater aquaculture in Cambodia in six provinces: Kandal, Kampong Thom, Siem Reap, Battambang, Pursat and Kampong Cham.
Minister Veng Sakhon thanked the United States Government for its support and contribution to the actual economic development of Cambodia through this project, demonstrating the United States' position in support of the Royal Government of Cambodia's policies.
The Minister stated that the Royal Government of Cambodia considers agriculture as a priority sector that plays an important role in contributing to the sustainability of Cambodia's economic growth, ensuring equity, food security and promoting national economic development.
He added that the aquaculture sector in Cambodia is showing signs of significant growth, and the sector also faces many challenges, such as the quality of juveniles that do not meet the requirements, the lack of access to technology and technology. Currently, hygiene requirements and phytosanitary standards and market conditions linking domestic and international markets are also limited.
At the same time, the Minister of Agriculture also requested the specialized units to increase the aquaculture technical capacity of fisheries officials and the general population in the target provinces to improve and help to disseminate to aquaculture of other provinces more widely to increase The potential of fishery products in terms of quantity, quality and reduced imports.
He added that each hatchery must strengthen its capacity to incubate more hatchlings with good quality for distribution to farmers to promote both rearing and no longer in the wild, as well as to raise Encourage the private sector to invest in increasing hatcheries, fish farms, fish feed factories, fishery product processing plants and so on.
The US Embassy Representative in Cambodia added that the CAST project will also help link trade between Cambodia and the United States.
It should be noted that this aquaculture development project for sustainable trade in Cambodia has received more than 17 million US dollars from the US Government to increase and develop freshwater aquaculture in Cambodia in six provinces, including: Kandal province. Kampong Thom, Siem Reap, Battambang, Pursat and Kampong Cham.
The project is directly funded by the US Department of Agriculture to run the program in line with the Royal Government of Cambodia's Strategic Aquaculture Development Plan. The project will help drive the growth of young fish, breeds and feed, as well as strengthen all relationships in the value chain from hatcheries to producers, buyers and distributors.
Cambodia will benefit from increased confidence and access to high quality fish and appropriate standards. In addition, the project will help the Cambodian economy by exporting fish to international markets and supplying quality local markets. Famous rice tycoon in Battambang province explains the situation of rice in Cambodia after posting a fictional video of farmers protesting (Video inside)
