APSARA Authority cooperates with Canada to excavate Buddhist temples in Angkor Thom
In the past, very little research has been done on Theravada Buddhist structures in Angkor, so the National University of Toronto, in collaboration with the Apsara Authority, conducted excavations to determine the structure. This building was built from the 13th to the 17th century AD.
How are the temples of Brahmanism and Mahayana Buddhism in the Angkorian period different from the medieval Theravada Buddhist structures of the transitional period?
Ear Darith, project coordinator representing the APSARA Authority, said that the findings confirm that Theravada Buddhism is very different in shape and size in Angkor Thom. These verses represent the beginnings of Theravada Buddhist construction in the Angkor area as well as throughout Cambodia. In the future, we need to make a list of these Buddhist shrines, categorize, excavate, research and compare with this type of construction in the area.
As a researcher with many collaborative international projects, Mr. Darith also thanked the National University of Toronto for its collaborative research with the APSARA Authority, for collaborating with international institutions to enable Cambodians to learn new techniques and skills. Disseminated Khmer culture, civilization, history, archeology and ancient Khmer temples to the world.
In addition, we can write co-published articles about the results of recent research. Through this work, national experts also have the opportunity to strengthen their ability to work on their own and continue to cooperate with the international community to increase good relations. Because research is always new, those results can contribute to a broader interpretation of Khmer history.
It should be noted that this is the third institution that has cooperated with the APSARA Authority to study the evolution of the structure of Theravada Buddhist temples after the French School of the Far East and the Nara Institute of Japan. There are 70 Theravada Buddhist temples in Angkor Thom, dating from the 13th to the 17th centuries.
Mr. Ho Sethy confirmed that the letter, called an instruction, was a forgery, not a cabinet letter that had been forged. At the same time, the director of Samdech Techo Hun Sen's cabinet also showed many clues that the letter was a fake letter.
According to Mr. Ho Sethy, the points that prove that the instructions are forged include:
* First: The Cabinet never issued instructions. Upon receiving the recommendation from the Prime Minister, the Cabinet shall make a letter to the Office of the Council of Ministers, which issues the instructions.
* Third: For real letters, the Cabinet always uses official yellow paper
* Fourth: There is no cabinet seal in the fake letter, if it is an official letter of the cabinet, it must be yellow paper and have the cabinet seal on it.
It should be noted that recently, a (false) instruction was distributed, alleging that it belonged to Samdech Techo Hun Sen's cabinet, causing confusion to the public and the people who follow the information.
Ministry of Industry, in collaboration with UNESCAP, conducts consultations on obtaining financing for small and medium enterprises and handicrafts
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Industry and Handicrafts in collaboration with the United Nations Economic and Social Commission for Asia and the Pacific (UNESCAP) this morning (January 31, 2019) organized a consultative workshop with stakeholders on access to finance. For small and medium enterprises and handicrafts in Cambodia.
On that occasion, Mr. Kim Touch said that today's workshop is an important forum to provide information updates as well as new policies that are the basis for the continued implementation of the work to encourage the enterprise sector. Small and medium and handicrafts in Cambodia.
He added that today's participants will also hear about the results of the study on access to finance for small and medium enterprises and handicrafts in Cambodia, as well as the input from all stakeholders to the results of this study. Accuracy is the basis for defining a new indicator in order to make it easier for small and medium enterprises and handicrafts to access financing.
At the same time, Mr. Chhea Lay Hy, Director of the Department of Small and Medium Enterprises of the Ministry of Industry and Handicrafts also said that currently there are more than 500,000 small and medium enterprises in Cambodia in all sectors, so the first challenge is the possibility. Access to finance is still difficult due to high interest rates and small business size, as well as a lack of trust from financial institutions.
