Minister of Commerce burns 25 tons of garlic containing chemical pesticides to protect people's well-being
Garlic imported from China containing more than 25 tons of pesticides and pesticides was destroyed by the General Department of CamControl of the Ministry of Commerce this morning. This Thursday, January 31, 2019, to protect the well-being of the people.
The incineration ceremony was held at Sihanoukville Landfill in O'Kambot Village, O'Oknha Heng Commune, Prey Nup District under the chairmanship of Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce and Mr. Yon Min, Governor of Preah Sihanouk Province. Invitation from the leaders and officials of the General Department of Camcontrol, local authorities and representatives of more than 40 relevant institutions.
On that occasion, the Minister praised the officials of CamControl Sihanoukville branch who removed all the dangerous garlic in time because this product can cause nervous system such as vomiting, diarrhea, convulsions. The eyes become narrow and difficult to breathe and can be fatal in severe cases.
The Minister added that today's incineration was in response to the concerns of the people and the high recommendation of Samdech Akka Moha Sena Padei Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of the Kingdom of Cambodia, which highlighted that "food safety issues in "Cambodia is the first of 12 negative imbalances that need to be addressed."
At the same time, Mr. Pan Sosak also requested all CamControl branch officials to continue their efforts to inspect the goods in the market and in the shops to participate in improving food safety for consumers in Cambodia in accordance with Approach program on "risk management" based on clear technical and scientific basis.
Please be informed that in 2018, the General Department of CamControl found and confiscated 66 tons of spoiled goods and unsafe food (vegetables, fruits, canned goods, seafood and fresh meat containing banned chemicals or pesticide residues). Incinerated, 5.3 tons of goods were confiscated from the border and at the market of Sihanoukville.
