Mr. Khieu Kanharith's commendation was made on the occasion of the inauguration of the statue of "Elder Phirum Vohar Prach Chhuon" in Wat Sam, Sam Village, Khvav Commune, Traing District, Takeo Province on the morning of January 29, 2019. The inauguration of the statue of Mr. Khieu Kanharith directly shows respect for Mr. Prach Chhuon, who was named by the people as the Emperor Chapey Dang Veng of Cambodia.
The statue of Prach Chhuon is made of sandstone, 0.8 meters wide and 1.20 meters high. The construction of this statue started from September 11 to December 18, 2018 and was completed in more than 3 months.
Prach Chhat, the son of Prach Chhuon, said that his father's statue was made of sandstone taken from the Preah Vihear temple and that the construction budget was fully sponsored by Khieu Kanharith. He said the statue cost $ 3,500.
According to Fresh News, Prach Chhuon's son also expressed his happiness to see his father's statue inaugurated to represent the Chapey scholars in Cambodia. He also did not forget to express his deep gratitude to all the people, especially to Mr. Khieu Kanharith, who fully supported the construction of the statue of Prach Chhuon.
Please be informed that the famous Cambodian elder Chapey Dang Veng, Mr. Prach Chhuon, passed away at about 1:30 AM on November 17, 2018 at the age of 82 years old.
Prach Chhuon was a student of a monk named At Sen in Wat Prom. Monks taught him Pali songs, poems and literary tales, which made him rich in rhetoric and literary knowledge.
After learning how to chase three long chapis, his first audience was the locals. Prach Chhuon is a chapei singer with a big, round voice, juicy and very talented.
Later, in 1962, he participated in the National Radio's "Competition for the Selection of Chapey Dang Veng" and won the competition with the first place among the 20 participants.
From then on, he started singing for the National Radio in Phnom Penh. The content of the song is determined by the radio station, which is often related to the political history and many celebrities in the United Nations.
After the fall of Democratic Kampuchea or the Khmer Rouge regime on January 7, 1979, Prach Chhuon resumed his career as a chapei singer. He returned to Phnom Penh and sang on the National Radio of Cambodia. In addition, he also received invitations to sing in other programs.
It should be informed that "Phirum Vohar Prach Chhuon" has 5 children, including 3 who live with him. Throughout his life, he held the chapei pole regularly. Due to his fame, he was invited to sing in some provinces and capitals such as Ratanakiri, Mondulkiri, Oddar Meanchey and Phnom Penh.
In addition, he has also performed abroad, such as in the United States twice, France three times and once in Singapore.
