Senior ministers from Portugal and South Africa say their government is open to Chinese investors and looks forward to expanding cooperation with Huawei, the country's largest telecommunications company. China.
The senior ministers have responded to growing concerns about Chinese investment in a number of developing countries, including Huawei. The government officials raised the issue in an interview with China Global Television (CGTN) in Davos, Switzerland, as they attended the annual meeting of the World Economic Forum.
"Portugal welcomes all investments, including Huawei's investment, without any discrimination," said Portuguese Economy Minister Pedro Shizavira.
"In Portugal, Huawei is a very important supplier for some telecom operators and our government has very good relations with companies from all over the world," said Pedro Shizavira, Minister of Economy of Portugal. "So we do not discriminate against any company."
"We also support the EU's concerns, but we see that there are a lot of opportunities that can be created," said Portuguese Economic Minister Pedro Shizavira. "And it is clear that China will remain a good friend of Portugal."
South African Minister of Transport Stella Anda Bernie Abraham says Huawei still has good relations with South Africa.
The minister added that the South African government has taken a number of measures to address the safety and security of Huawei's equipment.
