The Royal Government is preparing a draft law on "Public-Private Partnership Mechanism"
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Vongsey Visoth, Permanent Secretary of State of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, on behalf of Deputy Prime Minister Aun Porn Moniroth, Minister of Economy and Finance, on the morning of January 28, 2019, chaired a consultative workshop on Draft Law on "Public-Private Partnership Mechanism" for Public Investment Projects by Public-Private Partnership Mechanism (PPP)
The purpose of the seminar at the Phnom Penh Hotel is to gather more important input from representatives of relevant ministries, institutions of the Royal Government, development partners, investors from relevant sectors, as well as the National and International Chamber of Commerce to further improve this draft law. Comprehensive, consistent and can be implemented with high efficiency.
Mr. Vongsy Visoth stated that the draft law on public-private partnership mechanism is an important tool for managing the allocation and use of natural resources, both financial and human resources and intellectual resources more effectively in the facility. As Cambodia progresses, so does the demand for resources on public investment, especially in the field of infrastructure, while state resources are still limited.
He said that in order to jump to become a high middle-income country by 2030, Cambodia needs to increase public investment in about 40% of GDP per year, like other countries in the region, but the size of Cambodia's total public investment. Both public and private) currently account for only 28% to 29%, of which public investment accounts for only 8% of GDP.
Mr. Vongsey Visoth emphasized that although the mechanism of public-private partnership has its strengths and weaknesses, but the trend of almost all countries in the world is fully using this mechanism, including partners. Promote this work as well. Thus, the drafting of this law is a step towards building a transparent and fair framework for managing and sharing risks and benefits to attract more private investment.
At the same time, the most important thing is that Cambodia also needs to build a strong institutional capacity and governance system to strengthen the management of this mechanism, such as contract management and other issues to be able to ensure a win-win situation for all parties. States, investors and consumers, especially for nations and peoples.
He emphasized that in addition to attracting more public investment from the private sector, the Royal Government will also increase the transparency and efficiency of public investment expenditures by developing common guidelines for managing public investment projects in both national and sub-national budgets. National budget from partners and resources from the private sector.
