European powers ready to recognize opposition leader as Venezuelan president
NEW YORK (Reuters) - European powers have warned Venezuelan President Nicolas Maduro to hold new elections, otherwise they will recognize the opposition leader as legitimate president and Maduro has eight days to respond. . This was released by the BBC on Sunday, January 27, 2019.
However, Venezuelan Foreign Minister Jorge Arreaza told the crowd that Maduro was still the legitimate president and that his country would not hold re-elections under pressure. He also warned French President Francois Hollande that it would be better to take the time to deal with the yellow-shirt protesters than to interfere in Venezuela's internal affairs.
Nicolas Maduro was sworn in as his second president in early January after winning a non-partisan presidential election in March 2018. Top 10 news of the week for Sunday, January 27, 2019
