He added that the overall goal of agricultural development is to increase the export of quality and safe agricultural products by improving production productivity, adding value and expanding market competition, as well as maintaining the balance of agricultural land use. Sustainability.
At the same time, the Minister gave some suggestions to the ceremony as a vision for the implementation of sustainable rice production as follows:
Second, to establish sustainable rice production standards and set indicators and mechanisms to evaluate the quality to get a really good product recognized by the national and international markets.
Third, proper soil fertility must be managed to ensure sustainable production, economic efficiency and environmental balance through the use of a combination of natural and chemical fertilizers based on the type of soil and the actual needs of the crop. Try to grow alternative crops such as soybeans with rice to improve soil quality, increase soil fertility and reduce the damage of pollutants.
At the same time, it is necessary to apply better harvesting and post-harvesting techniques to reduce rice loss and improve the quality of rice in accordance with the standards that can get high prices in the market.
The Minister hopes that the new ideas, opinions and experiences from experts as well as stakeholders participating in this forum will be important input for the formulation of policies, master plans, agricultural development and measures as a solution in the future. Effective. Japan, UNICEF jointly announce end to all forms of violence against children with $ 2 million in Japanese aid
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation, the Royal Government of Japan and the United Nations Children's Fund (UNICEF) have announced the prevention and response to violence against children in Cambodia.
The new project aims to accelerate action to end all forms of violence against children in Cambodia. As a result, more than one million vulnerable Cambodian children will be better protected from violence.
This statement was made at the signing ceremony between the Royal Government of Japan and the United Nations Children's Fund (UNFPA) under the auspices of the Japanese grant of 2 million US dollars, with Mr. Vong Soth, Minister of Social Affairs as a witness. On January 22, 2019 at the Ministry of Social Affairs.
Under the auspices of the Japan Development Assistance Fund, amounting to US $ 2 million, this project will enhance the capacity of the Royal Government of Cambodia to accelerate the implementation of the Action Plan to Prevent and Respond to Violence against Children in 2017. Expand key interventions by 2021, test innovative measures and implement the INSPIRE framework, a seven-point strategy to effectively end violence against children.
Speaking at the signing ceremony, Mr. Vong Soth said that in order to contribute to the achievement of sustainable development goals, the Ministry of Social Affairs, Veterans and Youth Rehabilitation is leading efforts at the national level to strengthen the defense system. Children.
The Minister added that the establishment of a nationwide coordinated child protection system with skilled and competent staff will help children who have been subjected to violence, exploitation and abuse to receive care and treatment. The support they need will also help prevent future violence.
Mr. Hidehizabovinuji, Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary of Japan to Cambodia, acknowledged that child protection is one of the priority areas of the Government of Japan.
Hidehisa Horinoji said the need for child protection was included in the Sustainable Development Goals. The Japanese government supports various activities of the Sustainable Development Goals under the leadership of Prime Minister Abe.
He believes that this grant is useful for the good development of many children who will contribute to the future development of Cambodia.
Ms. Nata Chea Oson, Acting Director of UNICEF Cambodia, said that this project will continue to strengthen the capacity of the Royal Government of Cambodia to have a well-functioning and well-coordinated child protection system in place to prevent and respond to violence against children. More efficient.
The successful implementation of this project will make Cambodia a leading country in the region in the fight to end violence against children.
Cambodia is the first country in the Asia-Pacific region to conduct a national survey on violence against children in partnership with the US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) and UNICEF. The survey was first conducted in 2013, finding reliable data and national estimates that show the extent and nature of violence against children across the country.
In response to the findings of the 2013 Cambodia Violence Against Children Survey (CVACS), the Royal Government of Cambodia is committed to taking action on this issue and developing an action plan to prevent and respond to child violence in Cambodia. 2017-2021 (referred to as the action plan). Preah Sihanouk Provincial Commissioner Holds an Emergency Meeting to Disseminate Cooperation Plan for Strengthening Security and Public Order in the Province
