(Phnom Penh): Although pottery products are not very popular among modern consumers, but people in Anlong Russey village, Sre Thmei commune, Rolea Bier district, Kampong Chhnang province, which is known as a good clay area. The best in making pots is still continuing the career of this ancestor every day.
The pots and pans you are familiar with are not just a few, they come in many shapes and sizes and are very useful.
Anlong Russey village is not only a pottery factory, we wonder if that place can be visited by tourists? On the other hand, this special handicraft community also allows tourists from all walks of life to study, learn and buy items as well as souvenirs.
Mr. Seng Saly, Director of Kampong Chhnang Provincial Department of Tourism, said that in Kampong Chhnang province, there are two pottery handicrafts, one in Andong Russey village, Sre Thmei commune, Rolea Bier district, and the other in Panch Koul village, Chrey Bak commune, Rolea Bier district. .
Sitting on earthworks and other souvenirs with her two daughters under a decent house, Pov Samboun, a well-known artisan in Andong Russey village, briefly told the Fresh News team about her ancestral occupation. Tells about the production process, sales and some other related work as well.
Under the efforts of Kampong Chhnang Provincial Administration, Provincial Department of Tourism and some relevant institutions, the people in this bamboo pot production community have received continuous education and training from officials and civil society organizations, especially JICA of Japan, and have expanded their dissemination. From this pot production area as well.
With the efforts to promote the bamboo pot production community, the Director of the Department of Tourism also appealed to the people as well as international tourists to visit, explore and buy souvenirs and items from the pot production. Andong Russey village to do more to contribute to the promotion of national tourism, promote products that are inherited from the ancestors, as well as to improve the lives of people in this area.
