(China): China's top economic institutions have introduced a number of new measures to reduce the tax burden on companies, especially small and medium-sized companies.
"In total, there are 17.98 million companies that will be considered as small and medium-sized companies," said Xu Hongchai, China's deputy finance minister. "And these companies represent 95 percent of all tax obligations." Some experts believe that Chinese society now urgently needs the government to collect less taxes from companies.
By order of the Chinese Ministry of Public Security, police in the southern Chinese province of Guangdong, in cooperation with police in six other provinces in China, as well as Cambodian police, launched an operation to crack down on online gamblers late. Last September, two suspected ringleaders and 158 others were arrested.
To date, about $ 1.9 million of gambling and other funds have been confiscated and frozen by police, and 22 properties have been confiscated. Gambling-related activities have been shut down. A total of 91 suspects detained in Cambodia were deported to China on October 15.
"The crackdown is a sign of a large-scale crackdown on the arrogance of cross-border gambling gangs," said Zhang Xiaoling, deputy director of the security administration at the Ministry of Public Security. "The crackdown also contributes to the protection of China's overall economic stability and social security."
