The National Museum of China is located east of Tiananmen Square in Beijing, China. The mission of the museum is to educate the art and history of China. It is headed by the Ministry of Culture of the People's Republic of China. The museum opened on October 1, 1959.
Chunfa Wang, director of the National Museum of China, briefed the Prime Minister on a photo exhibition on China's 40 years of reform and opening up. That is, from the time China was led by President Teng Xiping to the present under President Xi Jinping.
The exhibition features photographs showing the activities of President Teng Xiuping and President Xi Jinping, who deepened their reforms to bring China out of a poor country to become the world's second largest economy. The world.
The exhibition of achievements in the country's development efforts is also open to Chinese and international guests.
