Cambodia reaffirms, tells Australia not to allow foreign troops on Cambodian territory
Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, made the remarks during a meeting with Scott Ryan this morning during his visit to Cambodia and the Asia-Pacific Parliamentary Forum in the province. Siem Reap, Cambodia.
Foreign Ministry spokesman Ket Sophan told reporters after the meeting that Scott Ryan had told the deputy prime minister that he had heard that Cambodia was allowing foreign military bases on the territory.
"I would like to reiterate that Cambodia does not allow foreign military bases, whether naval, land or air," he added. "We reject any information that distorts the facts." At the same time, the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia also sent a letter to the Vice President of the United States about not allowing foreign troops on its territory.
Mr. Scott Ryan stated this in a meeting with Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation on the morning of January 19, 2019, during his visit to Cambodia and attend the Asia-Pacific Parliamentary Meeting. Held in Siem Reap.
After the meeting, Mr. KET Sophan, Spokesman of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, briefed the media on the outcome of the special meeting. "His Excellency Scott Ryan confirmed that the Australian Government will continue to have strong and deep cooperation with the Royal Government of Cambodia as well as the Cambodian Parliament, especially in the field of education and tourism," he said.
In addition, the President of the Australian Senate also thanked and appreciated the well-organized Pacific Parliament with multicultural performances in Siem Reap.
During the meeting, Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon welcomed the presence of the current President of the Australian Senate. He informed the President of the Australian Senate about the election in Cambodia, which the CPP had won completely, while 20 political parties participated in the election.
According to Ket Sophan, during the meeting, the Deputy Prime Minister also expressed the Royal Government of Cambodia's commitment to serve the people, who are the voters, even though the CPP holds all the seats in the National Assembly. He informed Scott Ryan about increasing the Supreme Advisory Council to provide an opportunity for various parties to participate in the opinion to serve the people as well.
Regarding the current statement of the President of the Australian Senate, Mr. Sok Isan, the spokesman of the Cambodian People's Party, considered that Australia's position in cooperation with Cambodia for the new government is to provide justice to the people of Cambodia.
"It is fair to the Cambodian people that His Excellency the President of the Australian Senate, Ryan Scott, has pledged to continue to work closely and deeply with the Royal Government of Cambodia to become a friend," he said. "Trust each other."
He added that this is the pride of the Cambodian people to receive justice from the President of the Australian Senate, who has helped contribute to the search for peace for Cambodia in the past, unlike the slanderers.
