Mr. Sok Sopheak made this remark at a press conference on the actions of the Ministry of Commerce in response to the imposition of import duties on Cambodian rice, held at the Ministry of Commerce on the afternoon of January 18, 2019. This.
Hopefully, Cambodia will use the feature of Cambodian rice that European countries do not have: the quality of fragrant rice.
Seeing the gaps, he hopes the WTO dispute settlement system will work again so that Cambodia can protest over the tax issue.
The senior Commerce Ministry official's remarks came after some opposition groups called the European Union a tax on Cambodian rice as an EBA measure, which they have always insisted Europe use to put pressure on the Cambodian government. . However, the senior official claimed that the EBA was not involved, only to protect the interests of their member countries.
"This is not a big deal until there is a national surprise that the EU defense measures are politically motivated or otherwise, because there are a large number of countries in the world," he said. "As a member of the World Trade Organization (WTO), Europe has also used it in accordance with its national agreements and laws to protect the interests of domestic industries."
He attributed the tariff to the fact that Europe had increased its tariffs to 12 digits to bring Cambodian rice into a category similar to Italian rice, so Europe had resorted to deceptive techniques through its local regulations. They.
The Secretary of State considers that the imposition of tariffs on Cambodian rice exports has both good and bad consequences. The senior official suggested that the tax could cost Cambodia about $ 50 million. It may lower the price of rice for farmers when exporters have to raise prices to cope with the tax problem.
The good news is that Cambodia can reform quickly without waiting long, leading to Cambodia's economic independence without relying on tax incentives. Reforms to be introduced include: reducing costs for exporters and farmers by reducing the complexity and administrative costs, both formal and informal, reducing shipping costs, reducing electricity tariffs and a number of tax exemptions, including Reduce the cost of farmers' fertilizers, pesticides and so on.
At the same time, Cambodia needs to find new markets in addition to relying on Europe, including strengthening and expanding existing markets such as China, which provides a quota of 300,000 tons per year to Cambodia, but in 2018, Cambodia exported only about 170,000 tons. Other markets.
The European Union is an important market for Cambodian rice. In 2018, Cambodia exported nearly 300,000 tons of rice to Europe, equivalent to 42.98 percent of the total exported 620,000 tons.
