MOSCOW (Reuters) - The Russian Academy of Sciences (RAS) has agreed to develop a national program aimed at researching issues and solutions to space hazards, such as asteroids, comets and space debris. This is according to a quote from the Russian newspaper Sputnik on Friday morning, January 18, 2019.
According to RAS, researchers have found 18,000 potentially dangerous objects in space, 99 percent of which are asteroids. The number of potentially dangerous comets is only 0.1 percent, with the United States being the most active.
The head of the Russian space agency Roscosmos' strategic planning and special programs department also said that Russia should develop a national project to deal with such risks. In early 2013, Russia was hit by a massive asteroid that injured hundreds of people and damaged buildings.
