Ministry of Commerce calls EU rice tax decision as a weapon to kill Cambodian farmers and declares protest under international trade law
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Commerce of Cambodia has expressed its deep regret over the EU's decision to impose tariffs on Cambodian rice. The ministry described the move as a weapon to kill Cambodian farmers and declared its opposition to the resolution in accordance with international trade laws and regulations.
In a statement in response to EU action on the night of January 17, 2019, the Ministry of Commerce stated that the EU decision did not fully reflect aspects of international trade law, technical and factual norms and cooperation. Good past operations between the Royal Government of Cambodia, the private sector and the European Union.
In the statement, the ministry also stated that it was reviewing the decision based on international trade laws and regulations in order to continue to protest against the decision, which the Cambodian people perceived as unfair and socially incorrect. Economy.
The European Union (EU) has decided to start imposing tariffs on rice imports from Cambodia and Myanmar to 28 EU member states for three years from January 18, 2019. 2021.
The decision to impose tariffs on Cambodian rice comes as Europe finds that Cambodian and Myanmar rice imported into Europe has hurt their rice producers following complaints from Italy and Spain.
