(Stung Treng): Twelve families from Sre Krasaing and Koh Preah villages who are engaged in boating are urging the commune authorities as well as the governor of Siem Bok district, Stung Treng province, to help solve the problem of their boating business which was stopped while the boat owners New hands come running instead.
Mr. Yon Savien, a resident of Sre Krasaing village, a representative of the group of boats from Sre Krasaing village and Koh Preah, described on January 11 that they have been operating this boat for more than 10 years since 1998. They have 4 boats, the owners are divided into 2 groups, one group has 2 boats to transfer guests from Sre Krasaing to Koh Preah and the other group has 2 boats, the same is to transfer guests from Koh Preah to Sre Krasaing.
He added that every year, they always collect 6 million riels from the four boat owners to pay to the commune authorities. Suddenly, on January 7, a new business owner, Meng Leang Kry, came to copy the guests, confirming that he had a business license authorized by Mr. Thong Sran, the governor of Siem Bok district.
The owner of the boat, Kampong Dor, Koh Preah, also described that the authorities stopped them from operating the boat without prior notice, on January 7, also saw the presence of the boat. Recently, two Siem Bok district officials came to tell them to stop operating.
Therefore, the district administration met to decide on the work and also went to the partner, took a risk because of this request, try to see if it happens or not, but stopped within a year. This is not only for the benefit and reputation of the commune, but also for the profit and loss of business people.
He added that, therefore, on behalf of the local leaders, how to improve the lives of the people, according to Samdech Techo, always think of the people as a whole. According to the law relating to the provision of public services, there is no exclusive ownership, so the examination of what is better, better developed, can help alleviate the population.
The district governor added that he asked the 12 boat families to be ready for next year if they perform better than the current business owners.
