(Phnom Penh): Mr. Vongsey Visoth, Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, on January 15, 2019, stated that the Ministry of Economy is working hard to prepare for the reform of the internal economic system, which Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia recently announced a reduction in formal and informal spending to make it easier for investors and traders.
Mr. Vongsey Visoth made this assertion when he presided over the Public Forum on Macroeconomic Management and Budget 2019, which was held at the Ministry of Economy and Finance on the morning of January 15. 2019.
Mr. Vongsey Visoth stated that after Samdech Techo Hun Sen set the internal economic reform, now the Ministry of Economy and Finance is working hard to prepare for the reform of the internal economic system. Proceed quickly in accordance with the recommendations of Samdech Techo.
The introduction of the reform measures is aimed at curbing the impact on the Cambodian economy at a time when the European Union, a major market for Cambodia's exports, intends to withdraw preferential tariffs from Cambodia.
At the same time, Mr. Vongsey Visoth also explained that the introduction of the reform of the internal economic system will make Cambodia independent with self-reliance, but still do business with other countries without the same pressure as before. No.
Mr. Vongsy Visoth stated that "the reform of the internal economic system means that the internal system will become more competitive, but also more open to foreign countries."
(Phnom Penh): The Coalition of Cambodian Civil Society Organizations for Nutrition has organized its 2018 Annual Workshop with the aim of reviewing the progress and challenges in 2018 and to share in the discussion of the assessment of past progress from the experience. Achieved through the implementation of various SUN CSA projects, as well as identifying gaps, challenges and opportunities to establish best practice models and identify next steps for 2019.
The 2018 Annual Workshop of the Coalition of Civil Society Organizations for Nutrition of Cambodia was held on 14 January 2019 at the Phnom Penh Hotel, chaired by Mr. Sok Silo, Deputy Secretary General of the Council for Sector Rehabilitation and Rural Development and National Coordinator for the Nutrition Movement in Cambodia, relevant member organizations, ladies and gentlemen, national and international guests, and journalists.
Hou Kreun, Deputy Director of HKI and Coordinator of the Coalition of Civil Society Organizations for Nutrition in Cambodia, said that although Cambodia has made significant progress in improving the health and nutrition of mothers and babies in two years In the last decade, the problem of malnutrition in Cambodia has been of great concern to the Royal Government and all development partners.
He said that every year, nearly a third, or about 4,454 total child deaths in Cambodia, are linked to malnutrition. In addition, 32 out of 100 children under the age of five have stunting, an unacceptably high rate, and the trend of overweight and obesity is rampant in urban areas and Some rural areas, all of which are also a problem.
He added that in 2019, the Coalition of Civil Society Organizations for Nutrition in Cambodia will work closely with relevant ministries, especially the Council for Agricultural and Rural Development and members of the network to expand other nutrition interventions in Cambodia to improve in Joint efforts to eradicate all forms of malnutrition in Cambodia.
Mr. Sok Silo, Deputy Secretary-General of the Council for Agricultural and Rural Rehabilitation and National Coordinator for the Nutrition Movement in Cambodia, said that the movement aims to bring together all stakeholders, mobilize resources and share experiences and exchange ideas. To improve the nutritional status of the people of member countries.
