The Asia-Pacific Parliamentary Forum is a group of legislatures established to promote peace and sustainable development. APPF was established in 1993 in Japan and currently has 27 members. Cambodian head of state calls on Asia-Pacific nations to work together to strengthen peace, freedom, democracy, prosperity and resolve issues
(Phnom Penh): On the occasion of the 27th Asia-Pacific Parliamentary Forum (APPF-27) held from 13-18 January in Siem Reap, His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni, King of Cambodia The message calls on all Asia-Pacific nations to work together to find ways to strengthen the foundations of peace, freedom, democracy and prosperity, as well as to find solutions to problems in the future.
"We are entering a new era of international relations through the evolution of the world order, which is facing many complex and interconnected issues," he said. "No country can solve global problems alone, no matter how powerful it is."
"We need to establish international dialogue and cooperation to address issues such as climate change, natural disasters, poverty and inequality," he said.
"This is a wonderful time for all of us to come together to reflect on our past experiences and to anticipate our experiences," he said. "Major trends that will affect the Asia-Pacific region and anticipate future common ways to strengthen the foundations of peace, freedom, democracy and prosperity."
Multilateralism and the international legal system are also under threat due to the rise of unilateralism, protectionism, extremism, nationalism and populism in many countries around the world. .
"We need to be resilient and work together to promote a multilateral system that is open, legal and environmentally sound," he said. "We need to build a stronger international partnership to achieve the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals by 2030."
In this regard, the Kingdom of Cambodia, an active member of the international community, has integrated its national development strategy with the Sustainable Development Goals and is committed to contributing to international efforts to address these global issues. Preah Sihanouk Provincial Administration Builds Canal to Protect Kbal Chhay Area to Prevent Illegal Invasion
(Preah Sihanouk): Preah Sihanouk Provincial Administration, led by Mr. Srun Srun, Deputy Governor, led a joint force of armed forces and relevant professional officers to visit the canal to protect the Kbal Chhay area in accordance with the provisions of Sub-Decree No. 116 and (National Assembly). New government to block further encroachment.
In fact, on January 14, 2019, two (ESCA) vehicles were operating with the armed forces on guard. In the past, the Preah Sihanouk Provincial Administration had partially dug a canal to protect the Kbal Chhay area, but had to suspend it due to heavy rain.
Taking advantage of the suspension, anarchists looted and encroached, despite the Sihanoukville administration's ban on prevention and education, but they never listened to the instructions. And continue to invade the protected forest in this area.
Mr. Srun Srun, Deputy Governor of Preah Sihanouk Province, stated that this multi-purpose area of Kbal Chhay needs to be maintained because it is the only indispensable fresh water source for use throughout Preah Sihanouk Province.
Mr. Srun Srun urged those who entered the illegal area illegally, intentionally or at the instigation of anarchist ringleaders, to withdraw immediately. From now on, the Sihanoukville Provincial Administration will take action against the perpetrators without any understanding.
It should be noted that in the past, many families who have settled in Kbal Chhay area have been evacuated by the Preah Sihanouk Provincial Administration by providing clean water by providing plots of land with the policy of organizing lots with infrastructure. Roads, water, electricity for them to live legally.
These groups of people are known to have run to help collect fingerprints, protest to the national level to ask for intervention to occupy this land, even though they know that this place is designated as a protected area by the Royal Government And the case is banned.
However, the Sihanoukville provincial administration is firmly committed to protecting the Kbal Chhay freshwater area in accordance with the Royal Government's guidelines, at any cost.
