Here is the circular of the Ministry of Tourism: Discover: The progress of the space program behind the landing on China's moon
Last week, China became the third country to investigate the moon. But what is interesting is that China became the first country to study the other side of the moon, or so-called dark side, that people on the planet have never seen. The ability to land on the dark surface of the moon is a breakthrough for another Chinese technology that Russia or the United States has not yet done.
The Chang'e-4 space probe is symbolic of the progress of China's space program and the accumulation of vital capabilities for China itself and for ties with world powers. The news is making the United States hard to accept, as the US government, under Donald Trump, sees future space programs and space exploration as a major challenge.
One of the key drivers of US space policy throughout history was competition with Russia, especially in the context of the Cold War. If China's success continues to grow, what will the United States do?
Unlike the United States and Russia, the People's Republic of China first engaged in space activities during the development of ballistic missiles in the 1950s. While receiving some assistance from the Soviet Union, China successfully developed its own space program.
Recall that China launched its first satellite in 1970. The first manned spacecraft program was then suspended, shifting its focus to commercial satellites instead. This does not mean that China is careless in its efforts to become a space power.
Is this the era of renewed space racing?
The most notable feature of the Chinese space program, especially compared to the first US and Russian space programs, is its speed and stability. Due to the many secrets hidden around the space program, China's capabilities in space have not yet been clearly measured. However, it is concluded that China's ability on this program is unlikely to lose to its competitors.
