Canberra: Australian diplomat has expressed grave concern over US plans to withdraw troops from Afghanistan, while a former Australian military commander has voiced the ambiguity of Washington's commitment to the Middle East. Canberra should be urged to reconsider the alliance between the two countries. This was quoted from the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Monday afternoon, January 14, 2019.
In December, US media reported that US President Donald Trump was preparing to cut about 14,000 troops in Afghanistan by half. Around the plan, Australian Foreign Minister Marise Payne said it would be a matter of grave concern for Australia if its key partners decided to change their stance.
Meanwhile, Peter Leahy, who served as Australia's military chief from 2002 to 2008, said that due to the uncertainty surrounding the United States' future commitment to the Middle East, Australia should Reconsider the alliance between the two countries.
"If we are in a situation where we have made a strong commitment but the United States has changed their strategy almost entirely to Donald Trump, what should we do?" Leahy said. . "Australia needs to consider the credibility of the United States for a long time as an ally."
