"I became prime minister at the age of 32 and was in a state of peace and war." This is a reminder of the sad time of the leadership of the strong man of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen.
Starting a life as prime minister in a country that has just been liberated from the flames of war and disturbed by the flames of war along the border, as well as the economic blockade of neighboring countries, is uncivilized and severely lacking. .
The veteran prime minister's life as a veteran of the countless battles and struggles has been fraught with difficulties as the country has been economically besieged by liberal nations and disrupted by the military's attempt to retake the country. Remnants of the Khmer Rouge on the Khmer-Thai border.
The 1980s was a time when Cambodia struggled to overcome all obstacles. Cambodia is considered to be the poorest country in Southeast Asia, and its people are even poorer, relying on a small amount of rice farming and trade, with no major trade.
Apart from the economic problems, there are many other issues such as education, training, human resources and border explosions are all issues that the young Prime Minister has to deal with.
Despite tens of thousands of chapters, the young Prime Minister worked hard to overcome obstacles and solve a series of problems, leading to peace talks with Cambodians along the border, especially with His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk, until an agreement was reached. Paris, October 23, 1991 and led to the election and the re-establishment of the Kingdom of Cambodia.
Under the clear vision that ending the war on the border is important for bringing about national development, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, also launched a plan and implemented a win-win policy by opening negotiations. With the Khmer Rouge until the Khmer Rouge resistance forces took refuge under the roof of the same Royal Government, leading to the complete closure of the country's armed forces on December 28, 1998.
Starting from the end of the civil war, Cambodia has had full opportunities for national development. For 33 years under the leadership of the Cambodian navy, Samdech Techo Hun Sen made Cambodia, the poorest country in 1985, a low-middle-income country today. Cambodians, who had no income in 1985, now have an average income of about $ 1,435.
Cambodia in 1985, with a population of just over 5 million, is becoming a country with a population of almost 16 million today.
Phnom Penh, with few residential buildings left over from the war of 1985, is becoming a capital with many high-rise commercial buildings. According to the report of the Ministry of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction, there are 979 high-rise projects from 5 floors up in Phnom Penh.
In addition to the sectors mentioned above, it is also seen that many important sectors of Cambodia are growing significantly compared to 1985 and now. Looking back, Cambodia's economy is also growing remarkably, maintaining a growth rate of around 7% for the last decade or so.
