International Analysis: Why is the withdrawal of US troops from Syria beneficial for Iran?
Of course, the withdrawal of troops from Syria seems to be in line with one promise, but it also contradicts another promise: he allowed Iran to expand its influence in the Middle East. Moreover, he made the statement in January, declaring that Iran "can do whatever it wants on the territory of Syria."
The area in northeastern Syria, where the United States has deployed its troops, is also the area that Iran aspires to control most. It is rich in oil resources and near the border with Iraq, where Iran has sent Iraqi allied PMF forces to Syria under the pretext of clearing ISIS. Iran wants to occupy the area to facilitate access to the Syrian-Iraqi border.
Previous US military presence in the area has hampered the freedom of movement of militants. It also prevents Iranian forces from gaining control of the region's oil fields. In the absence of a US military presence in the area, Iran is taking advantage of oil sales in the region to offset some of the economic losses caused by sanctions after the US withdrew from the nuclear deal.
The withdrawal of US troops will give Iran both time and space to strengthen its presence and access to resources, and ultimately make it harder for Russia to pull Syria out of Iranian control.
The withdrawal of US troops could also give ISIS an opportunity to resume its activities. The withdrawal could also be the end of the Syrian Democratic Forces and Kurdish forces, most of which live on Syrian territory and are allies of the coalition in the fight against ISIS. They will withdraw from the fight against ISIS in the absence of US support and they will be vulnerable to attacks by Turkey, which has always considered them a terrorist group.
Such a new evolution would pave the way for ISIS to concentrate and rebuild. After ISIS escalated, it became an excuse for Iran to increase the number of troops sent to Syria to fight ISIS without being blocked by the US military presence.
