The eight proposals that Samdech Techo Hun Sen raised in the third aid with the media on January 11, 2019 include:
1: Media institutions, both public and private, must act as true media institutions, be a mirror of the real society and do not act as a mirror to spread false information from society.
2. All public and private media institutions must disseminate factual information with clear explanations to the people to better understand the direction and policy of the Royal Government and bring back the truth from the people to the government. Both at the design level and the level of effective implementation.
3. Ask the media to help criticize to build on all the mistakes and plans set out by the ministries and institutions to improve, and not to criticize just to sink.
4: Request for journalists to study and analyze internationally to know about things that are happening in the world and also want journalists to become analysts for foreign events because in the past there were foreigners Analyze and broadcast from Cambodia. Therefore, Cambodia must know how to study, interpret and analyze from them as well.
5: Request the dissemination of all information properly, professional ethics. In particular, Samdech Techo requested the users of the social network Facebook (Facebook) not to insult, tear up each other, which leads to lawsuits and lawsuits. He urged Facebook users to keep their values, especially journalists, to be clear, unpredictable and not to act as a court.
6: Participate in eliminating journalists who extort money, do not be journalists just for extorting money from others or as a "mafia" news group.
7: Do not produce and help prevent fake information that does not benefit society. On the other hand, in order to combat fake news, journalists need to release real information and interpret it in order to eliminate it.
8. Efforts must be made to enhance the capacity of journalistic staff and to train human resources and develop the media. In particular, contact with associations, institutions, universities and the Ministry of Information to receive training in journalism skills.
