Preah Vihear Provincial Department of Culture rejects images of vandalism on temple walls in Preah Vihear province from the 1990s
(Preah Vihear): The Department of Culture and Fine Arts of Preah Vihear Province has issued a letter denying the destruction of statues on the walls of Banteay Khang temple in Ta Seng village, Ranasir commune, Sangkum Thmei district, in the area of Bakan temple (Preah Khan) in Preah Vihear province. This is not true at the moment, this image dates back to the 1990s, but is now carefully protected by the relevant authorities.
The Preah Vihear Provincial Department of Culture and Fine Arts issued a letter of denial after some media outlets reported that crooks had stolen statues at the temple, causing the general public to be confused and accuse the relevant authorities of not Pay attention to the task of protecting the national heritage as well.
Please note that images that have been destroyed since the 90s are not recent. At present, the temple area is protected and preserved by no less than 30 conservation forces and heritage police stationed there.
At the same time, Mr. Neou Chankong, Director of the Department of Culture and Fine Arts of Preah Vihear Province, confirmed to Fresh News earlier that in some cases, the media reported that a criminal had stolen a statue on the wall at Bakan Temple. Khan) is a picture from the 90s and completely rejects the recent publication of some media.
He added that the images that were recently published were only edited and misplaced to attack the management of the Department of Culture and relevant authorities, he added. Here, no one trades artifacts like during the war.
