Mr. Thao Sok Mony, Undersecretary of State, Ministry of Commerce, said at the seminar on "Business Development in Rural Areas in Cambodia" held on January 10, 2019 that the online trade law compiled by the Ministry of Commerce It has been up for many years now, while it is currently under scrutiny in the Cabinet.
"The introduction of this e-commerce law to promote local products to a wider market means that there are about 16 million people in Cambodia, but if we sell ordinary goods, only 16 million, but if "Online will sell more, not only locally, but also abroad."
He added that the introduction of the e-commerce law is best for Cambodia because it benefits both sellers and buyers, meaning that when the law is officially issued, sellers will not be able to deceive buyers with other things, such as: Unlike pictures, goods are not quality, there is no responsibility for shipping to customers send money but do not send goods and so on. The same goes for buyers, it is impossible to deceive the seller by any means.
"If the e-commerce law is discussed by the cabinet at a technical level, it will be released in 2019," he said.
Dr. Ky Sereywat, an economist and president of the Chinese Studies Center at the Royal Academy of Cambodia, said that online commerce is important, but many rural people do not yet know how to use the Internet, so it should be. Local trainers to train you on how to use this internet system.
He added that in order for the business to run smoothly and successfully, all stakeholders should find ways to share information more widely, find storage space, delivery systems, payments and so on.
Mr. Nguon Meng Tech, Director General of the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce, said that from the beginning until now, business in Cambodia has relied on cash payments and have to go directly to the location of these goods, these factors cause loss of time, money and Provide risk factors for sellers and buyers.
