After talks with US, Chinese Ministry of Commerce expects to resolve all concerns and trade wars
BEIJING (Reuters) - Trade talks in Beijing with US trade officials have laid the groundwork for continuing to find solutions to issues of mutual concern over the war, the Chinese Ministry of Commerce said on Thursday. Trade. This was released by Channel News Asia on Thursday afternoon, January 10, 2019.
In a statement, the Chinese Ministry of Commerce said: "Trade talks in Beijing with US trade officials have laid a solid foundation for further solutions to issues of mutual concern. "... Not only that, the two sides also agreed to continue to maintain closer contact."
But at the same time, US officials want Beijing to buy more US products to address the trade deficit, as well as increase openness to foreign investors in the Chinese market.
US officials paid a visit to Beijing from Monday to Wednesday to attend trade talks with China for the first time since Xi Jinping and Donald Trump agreed last month. Last December, a three-month moratorium on trade wars broke out.
