(Phnom Penh): On the morning of January 9, 2019, Camcontrol officers at the end of Phnom Penh dry port inspected a handicraft shop in Sangkat Boeung Kak I, Khan Toul Kork, Phnom Penh, after receiving complaints from residents that handicrafts This has no date and is not legal in Cambodia.
Kong Vuthy, deputy director of the Phnom Penh Dry Port Camcontrol branch, said while inspecting the handicraft that the tofu handicraft was named Chung Kour Shanghai and produced tofu without the correct date and brand. For example, put Chinese characters above Khmer characters.
He said that during the search, his team did not arrest the owner of the craft, but only instructed the owner to correct their brand name and date the product and expire in accordance with the law. In Cambodia, and if the instructions once and the owner still does not follow will take action in accordance with Cambodian law in force.
"After the search, we told the owner to set a specific date, location, place of manufacture to facilitate inspection in the markets that are sold in Cambodia," he said.
At the same time, Mr. Kong Vuthy also called on the owners of various handicraft products to set the date of their products and the place of production in accordance with the law before the CamControl officers inspected in 2019.
"These activities are in accordance with the instructions of His Excellency the Head of the Royal Government Delegate in charge of the General Department of CamControl and the President of Camcontrol in Phnom Penh," he said.
