The new 112-storey, 132-room Monk Hospital, a Chinese grant, is under construction and is scheduled to be completed in three years.
(Phnom Penh): The director of Preah Kosomak Hospital, Dr. Tan Sokun, said that the new building of Preah Kosomak Hospital, called the Lok Sang Hospital, which has 11 floors and 132 rooms, is being built gradually after opening the construction site. On October 30, 2018.
This statement was made by the director of Preah Kosomak Hospital during the inspection of the construction site of this new building on January 8, 2019, with the participation of representatives of the Ministry of Health and Chinese donors.
He said the new hospital building would take 40 months to build, but the Chinese side promised to speed up the construction of the building within 36 months before the contract.
At present, Lok Sang Hospital has 350 beds and a total of 563 staff, including 231 women, 40 contract staff and 74 workers. Ministry of Economy announces tax incentives for small and medium enterprises in priority sectors
The purpose of this workshop is to inform the public, especially small and medium enterprise owners, to be informed and to take advantage of the tax incentives that the Royal Government has introduced to promote the development of small enterprises. And medium-sized enterprises (SMEs), which are important drivers of economic development, job creation and increasing domestic production capacity.
In parallel, the Royal Government has established the Skills Development Fund, the SME Bank, the Entrepreneurship Development Fund and the Entrepreneurship Promotion Center.
Priority areas for providing tax incentives include:
1. Agricultural or agro-industrial products. 2. Food production and processing. 3: Manufacturing that produces domestic consumer goods, recycling waste, and producing goods for tourism. 4. Manufacturing that produces finished products or parts or components to supply other industries. 5. Research and development of information technology. Including the provision of innovative information technology management services.
6. Enterprises in the area of small and medium enterprises, and enterprises that develop this area.
Please note that these priority areas do not include areas that affect social security, health and the environment.
Small and medium enterprises in the above-mentioned sectors can receive incentives such as:
1: Exemption from income tax for 3 years from the date of tax registration for newly registered enterprises or from the date of enterprise update for enterprises that have already registered for tax from the date of entry into force of this sub-decree. .
2: Exemption from income tax for 5 years from the date of tax registration for newly registered enterprises or from the date of enterprise update for enterprises that have already registered for tax from the date of this sub-decree. Applicable to any of the following conditions: Use local raw materials from 60% up or increase the number of additional employees by at least 20% or located in the SME Cluster.
3. Exemption from income tax exemption and minimum tax during the income tax exemption period.
4: Encourage deductible expenses as follows: A: Allow 200% deduction for expenses on the use of accounting records through information technology systems, B: Allow 200% deductions for training expenses Maintain accounting or technical skills training for employees, and c: Allow 150% deduction for investment in innovative machinery or equipment to serve and improve productivity.
The Ministry of Economy and Finance thanked for providing more good inputs for further consideration and requested the private sector, associations of small and medium enterprises and stakeholders to find out and help disseminate the guidelines. This policy encourages all small and medium enterprises to have the opportunity to reap the maximum benefits as stated in the sub-decree above.
