Brigadier General Thi Chhay, Commander of the Chbar Morn Municipal Gendarmerie, expressed his gratitude and appreciation for the excellent donations for public security work in the city and throughout the province. Researcher revives Chinese classical music (Video inside)
(China): A Chinese researcher has spent a large part of his life exploring Chinese classical music and reviving it to attract modern attention.
Mr. Tian Qing is the president of the Chhun Chu and Pin Chen Drama Study Association. He is also the former director of the China Intangible Cultural Heritage Protection Center. Thean says his search for Chinese classical music began decades ago when he began his career in classical music.
At the time, he was a teacher of Chinese classical and Western classical music at the Tianjin Conservatory of Music. It was at that time that he discovered that there were very few documents related to Chinese classical music compared to the number of documents on Western classical music.
"I went inside the temple to see if there was any classical music there," said Tian Qing, an expert on intangible cultural heritage. Religion-related cultures are rarely studied. But at that time, no restricted area was set. Researchers generally do not enter the study area. So I walked into the area without hesitation.
Holding a recorder in his hand, Thean began studying religious music 30 years ago and has continued to do so ever since. He spent 10 years recording Buddhist music, and the 30 newspapers he made became a treasure trove for the next generation to study Chinese classical music.
"I have gained a lot of knowledge and understanding of the importance of ancient Chinese culture, such as Buddhist culture, which is far more unique than we think," said Tian Qing. "This culture has maintained its survival very well, even though it has gone through many difficult circumstances."
Thean was appointed vice president of China's Intangible Cultural Heritage Protection Center in 2006. He organized an exhibition to showcase the results of the nation's efforts to preserve culture. The exhibition provides an opportunity for Chinese people to revisit the nation's precious cultural heritage.
Located south of the Taklimakan Desert, Lop County is on the list of districts stuck in China's national poverty. But with the help of modern equipment and technology, pigeon farms in the district can produce pigeons every 35 days.
"If a family can produce a baby pigeon, preventing epidemics and finding markets will not be able to meet demand," said Adak Yasin, first secretary of the Calvary Village Party Committee. "So we formed a group of 19 families stuck in poverty, and we set one of the families to lead the pigeon farm."
According to Yasin, the village is using collective breeding pigeon formulas and has set up several collective support groups to develop pigeon farms. In each group, there are many experts to cover the work of breeding pigeons, while the villagers can work on the farm or can become a shareholder of the farm to earn more income.
In Calvary, 166 families are living in poverty, and most of them are shareholders in the pigeon farm today. The collective aid group also contracts with large local pigeon farmers, who buy the pigeons from the farmers and sell them collectively to ensure that farmers have a regular income.
"Our company is responsible for preventing the spread of disease, providing technical training and providing joint guidance," said Zhang Ying, general manager of Lop County Prefecture. "Through the combination of the five, we can guarantee the quality of all the pigeons we transport from the farm and can guarantee a product that is standard and high quality."
