Take a look at the quiet and unforgettable memories in Phnom Penh after the liberation on January 7, 1979 and the current scene
(Phnom Penh): January 7, 2019 Tomorrow is the 40th year that Phnom Penh has been liberated from the genocidal regime of Pol Pot. Phnom Penh, which used to be crowded and crowded before April 17, 1975, was transformed into a quiet state under the Democratic Kampuchea regime of Pol Pot, Ieng Sary, Khieu Samphan and Nuon Chea on April 17. 1975 to 7 January 1979.
This regime, after fighting to defeat Lon Nol's regime of the Khmer Republic for only a few hours, they gathered all the people in Phnom Penh to go down to the rice fields to grow, achieve great ambitions, great transformation, a great country. Only on agriculture.
The policy of evacuating people from the city to the rice fields of the Democratic Kampuchea regime, also known as the Killing Fields regime for 3 years, 8 months and 20 days, has made Phnom Penh, a hub of economy, culture and tourism, a quiet place. The valley, which some people call a ghost town because it is uninhabited.
After the genocidal regime was overthrown by patriotic forces under the banner of the United Front for National Salvation on January 7, 1979, some people began to return to Phnom Penh. After the day of liberation, it was said that Phnom Penh had only about 70 people.
Forty years after the fall of the Killing Fields regime, Phnom Penh is gathering more than 2 million people and more than 1 million people who come to do business and study. . At the same time, they also observed the expansion of the area of the capital with the increase of small and large buildings high and low.
