These three individuals are seen as the pillars of democracy today, as they control influential networks that are critical to democratic societies around the world.
As a result, Facebook has been widely criticized for allegedly helping Russia influence the 2016 US presidential election. But using social media to do this is not unique to the United States. Ukraine, Germany, Italy, Poland, France have all tasted it, and even Brexit (the process of removing Britain from the EU) has been influenced by social media distortions.
Affecting a democratic society Today, social media is threatening to destroy the democratic society that gave birth to them. Initially, social media was hoped to help spread democracy around the world, but it was used by groups to spread false information.
Democracy is increasingly reflecting the will of the media. In particular, these social networks have a limited influence on the public's perceptions, opinions and decisions in politics, so they also influence policies and leadership, in general, they are the decision makers of political power.
Recognizing the dangers of spreading false information and the potential for damage to its image and revenue, social networks such as Facebook have begun partnering with content review companies in 24 countries, but have yet to confirm the results. The effectiveness of this project yet. Each country also has a responsibility to establish monitoring mechanisms to prevent information manipulation.
