Letters from readers: The future of Cambodian bamboo
(Phnom Penh): At the age of 60, Bly, who had lived through the past of war and genocide like many other Cambodians in that generation, was fortunate to survive to this day. There is a strong desire that Cambodian children, especially the younger generation, remember all the important stories of the history of their homeland.
Evidence questions that are common in our society today: Why does a young person not see the opportunity that is causing him to study with confidence and hope for his future? Why does a young person allow himself to fall into a trance, forget about the future, and then place the blame on social leadership?
Why does a young man from a rural area working in a luxury hotel in Phnom Penh not pay the state price, which helps ensure a decent job and wages for him? Why does a young man who claims to be an independent social analyst repeat the inaction to attract other young people to lose hope in government leadership?
Why did a young man who grew up overseas join the open opposition to the local authorities who once saved their parents from genocide and had the opportunity to move abroad? ?
Why do politicians who claim to be the most patriotic and democratic try to persuade the younger generation to forget the past, forget the historical truth, forget the gratitude of the previous generation and incite them to join the social change movement towards a future in Unrealistic fantasies?
The stories of the past often fade away as time passes quickly if one does not strive to preserve memories. Today's young Cambodians under the age of 40, who are the heirs of bamboo in the future, should study and understand in order to truly remember the ancient history of their homeland, that is, from the time before they knew the sun.
Koh Kong CamControl Officers Find Some Out-of-Date Drinks at Markets and Residents in Thmar Bang District
(Koh Kong): For the safety and health of people living in mountainous areas, Mr. Huy Phearum, Chief of CamControl Branch, Koh Kong Province, led the subordinates in cooperation with commercial officers on January 4, 2019, Cam Kong Branch, Koh Kong Province inspected all aspects. Goods that traders display in the market and at homes in Russey Chrum commune, Thmar Bang district, Koh Kong province.
At the same time, CamControl officials also distributed signs to promote to nearly 500 students to understand the goods beyond the date of production should not be bought to eat.
Mr. Huy Phearum, Branch Manager of CamControl in Koh Kong Province, said that the operation was based on the Law on Quality Control, Safety of Products and Services and Trade Facilitation through Risk Management. The outside of the food to the label, the packaging, the date, the bread, the rotten bulge, etc.
He added that at the same time, to check the quality and compliance by conducting a preliminary analysis of the product using a test kit to find banned chemicals such as porcine and formaldehyde, in case of suspicion to take samples to send. Further analysis in the laboratory of the General Department of CamControl to determine the risk.
Mr. Huy Phearum confirmed that for the expired drinks, the branch officials confiscated them and stored them at the branch for destruction, and the professional officers advised the traders not to buy anything that was out of date or close to the expiration date. Come and sell it.
