(Champasak-Laos): Although not yet able to grow coffee according to organic standards, Lao farmers in Champasak province have a special technique that is different from coffee farmers in Cambodia, that is, growing mixed crops or so-called "agricultural crops" in the field. Its coffee, which has really benefited the coffee plant, the livestock and the farmers themselves.
Such features were revealed through a four-day visit to Champasak province organized by CEDAC in late 2018.
In a large coffee plantation in Paksong district, Champasak province, Mr. Oum Sabunsay Borkeo, a well-known farmer and owner of the plantation, described that Lao farmers in Paksong district have received cultivation techniques. Coffee from some international organizations.
On the other hand, growing a variety of plants such as vegetables, fertilizers, cantaloupe under the coffee tree also provides a special natural fertilizer for the roots and coffee plants, with this special technique was the practice of Lao farmers in the past.
In relation to this method, Cambodian CEDAC experts are also very interested, stating that they will bring these good techniques to disseminate training to Cambodian farmers so that Cambodian farmers can try to follow the aim. Let their coffee grow well and provide additional benefits to farmers themselves.
What is even more special is that as the market demand of Lao farmers' coffee in Paksong district continues to increase from year to year, Lao farmers will help to promote Cambodian organic coffee, especially in Mondulkiri province and will cooperate in both marketing techniques. Out and related work with CEDAC closer as well.
