(Phnom Penh): Three women living in Banteay Meanchey province have asked the Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara, Minister of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction, to intervene in the case of Borey Raksmey Duong Chan failing to transfer land and house use rights and not issuing ID cards. The right to occupy the real estate to the buyer and not to build the house completely according to the sale and purchase agreement.
The three women allege that the fraudulent business was carried out by the "Borey Raksmey Duong Chan" company in the picture above, which caused a lot of damage to the interests and property of the people.
Believing the company's claims and consolation, the victim gave a thumbs up to the new deal. But so far, the company has not handed over the new agreement, on the pretext that the company's lawyer in Phnom Penh has not signed the new agreement, so the victim does not know what the conditions are. In the new agreement.
Suddenly, on October 13, 2018, the victim handed over $ 20,000 to the company's representatives, but they refused to accept, requiring a fine of more than $ 13,000. More. The victim does not know what the fine is because there is no new agreement in hand yet.
However, Fresh News will wait for clarification from the "Borey Raksmey Duong Chan" regarding the allegations of the three customers until the intervention of Deputy Prime Minister Chea Sophara, Minister of Land Management, Urban Planning and Construction.
Please note that Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, has decided to support workers during childbirth since the beginning of 2018. Among them, one maternity worker received 400,000 Riels, two twins received 800,000 Riels and three twins received 1.2 million Riels.
The workers expressed great satisfaction with the support of the Royal Government, as this budget has contributed a large part to solving their difficulties. In addition to this subsidy, the labor law in Cambodia has another feature: workers during childbirth, they can take three months off with a rate of 120 percent per month.
Through this support and the above labor law, the Royal Government pays high attention to the well-being of workers in the textile, garment and footwear sector.
