MOSCOW (Reuters) - US President Donald Trump's economic policies, especially the trade war with China in 2018, have shaken the geopolitical world economy, with some countries continuing to spend the US dollar as an international currency. Five countries are trying to abandon the dollar. These five countries are:
Trump's trade war over raising tariffs on Chinese goods has forced Beijing to take countermeasures to keep China, the world's second-largest economy, from relying too heavily on the US dollar. Too. These measures include appreciating the yuan in trade with partner countries, especially the appreciation of the yuan through political, economic and lending programs.
In 2018, in addition to China, US European allies, including India, were also hit by trade due to Donald Trump's new policy. In fact, to reduce the impact of the dollar, New Delhi has used the national currency, the rupee, to buy S-400 missile systems from Russia and to buy oil from Iran.
Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan in 2018 announced plans to stop spending US dollars on trade with partner countries such as China, Russia, Ukraine and Iran. The announcement comes after relations between Ankara and Washington have soured since the Turkish coup in 2016.
Following the announcement of Donald Trump's withdrawal from the Iran nuclear deal in 2015, the United States has decided to re-impose economic sanctions against Tehran. The sanctions are aimed at Iran's oil sector, and Tehran has responded by spending Iranian money instead of the US dollar on its oil-buying partners.
