According to the report, 2,645 helmets and goggles were inspected on January 2, 150 education helmets and 350 glasses were fined.
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Justice will organize a roundtable discussion on "The Road to Success of Young People in Legal and Judicial Careers" on January 18, 2019 at the Ministry of Justice.
The forum aims to raise awareness of the roles and responsibilities of each profession, including judges, prosecutors, clerks, bailiffs, notaries and lawyers, and the readiness of young people to become legal and judicial professionals. This is according to the announcement of the Ministry of Justice, which was received by Fresh News on the afternoon of January 2, 2019.
The statement said that the roundtable was attended by professional speakers to share experiences and educate the public, with the coordination of Mr. Chin Malin, Undersecretary of State, Ministry of Justice and Chairman of the Permanent Secretariat of the Steering Committee for Legal and Judicial Reform.
(China): China's e-commerce law, which comes into force, requires sellers to have a business license to provide their services. These changes are to better protect the rights of users.
The new e-commerce law came into force on January 1, 2019, after being approved by the Chinese parliament on August 31.
This law aims to "protect the rights and legitimate interests of all parties" and "maintain market order". This law requires all operators in the e-commerce system to fulfill their obligations to protect the rights and interests of users as well as personal information, intellectual property, protection against hacking in the Internet and the environment.
Kor Jiangjiao, a professor at the Faculty of Law at Futan University in Shanghai, says the new e-commerce law incorporates more and more online merchants into e-commerce. He added that by increasing the opportunities for more companies to enter the online market, it will create a better environment for consumers.
"It establishes a common code of conduct that governs both online and offline shopping," said Kor Jiangjiao, a professor at Futan University Law School. "Online merchants such as WeChat sellers, overseas buyers and sellers of live video are all subject to this provision."
In addition, the cost of services will increase as the new law requires agents to purchase, register and pay taxes for providing their services to consumers. (China's CCTV)
