(Phnom Penh): Owners and representatives of some organic cafes in Cambodia, such as Bousra Cafe, are interested in combining Cambodian Robusta coffee in Mondulkiri with Lao Arabica coffee in Champasak to enhance the taste. Consumers as organic coffee lovers in Cambodia are growing.
This new key was raised after the owner and representative of the above organic coffee ended his visit with CEDAC at the coffee plantation in Champasak province, Laos at the end of 2018.
On his way back to Phnom Penh, Mr. Bou Panha, a representative of Busra Coffee, revealed that during his visit to Champasak, he tasted a new coffee that combines Robusta coffee in Mondulkiri with Arabica coffee in Champasak. Said it really tastes weird.
New, because he does not drink as much coffee as others, but this resident of Banteay Chhmar does not have any symptoms of intoxication or coffee poisoning that he made by hand, which indicates that there is no reaction to the two types of coffee, on the contrary. It gives a satisfying new flavor.
That is why he is interested in combining Robusta coffee in Mondulkiri with Arabica coffee in Champasak, Laos to give his customers a new flavor. He said that currently there are coffee shops in Cambodia also combine these two types of coffee to sell to customers.
What is some of the gaps for coffee in Champasak is that although they do not use chemicals, farmers there do not know how to grow coffee to organic standards and quality certification from any institution. On the other hand, despite the abundance of Arabica coffee, there is also a shortage of Robusta coffee.
It should be informed that in Cambodia, most of them are rich in Robusta coffee, which is grown according to CEDAC organic standards in Mondulkiri province, such as in Busra commune, Pichreda district and Dak Dam commune, Oreang district, which is only about 900 meters high, while Arabica coffee is not yet There is still a lot of this, despite the altitude and the unfavorable climate for Arabica coffee.
